Exactly ;) The moment you are there, “nothing” already has a witness.

Even alone, you’re breathing, sensing, remembering, anticipating, occupying time, changing state. So literal nothing would require:

\text{no actor}+\text{no experience}+\text{no change}

—which means there’d be nobody around to call it nothing.

So “doing nothing” is really shorthand for doing nothing in particular.

And with Frog and Toad, it gets even better: two people can jointly choose no objective, which turns the absence of an objective into a shared act.

\text{nothing alone}=\text{unstructured being}

\text{nothing together}=\text{shared unstructured being}

The blank space still has content. ;)